1f:["$","section","1-One transcript can produce 3.67 and 4.17",{"className":"article-section","id":"section-2","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"One transcript can produce 3.67 and 4.17"}],[["$","p","Consider three equally credited courses: regular English A, Honors Chemistry B and AP History A. Base points of 4, 3 and 4 total 11, so unweighted GPA is 11 ÷ 3 = 3.67. With example bonuses of 0.5 and 1.0 on the two advanced courses, the total becomes 12.5 and weighted GPA is 4.17.",{"children":"Consider three equally credited courses: regular English A, Honors Chemistry B and AP History A. Base points of 4, 3 and 4 total 11, so unweighted GPA is 11 ÷ 3 = 3.67. With example bonuses of 0.5 and 1.0 on the two advanced courses, the total becomes 12.5 and weighted GPA is 4.17."}],["$","p","The grades have not changed. The extra 0.50 in the displayed GPA comes from the selected bonus policy. Change the policy or the credits and the result changes.",{"children":"The grades have not changed. The extra 0.50 in the displayed GPA comes from the selected bonus policy. Change the policy or the credits and the result changes."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined",["$","nav",null,{"className":"answer-links","aria-label":"Next steps for One transcript can produce 3.67 and 4.17","children":[["$","$L4","/tools/weighted-gpa-calculator/",{"href":"/tools/weighted-gpa-calculator/","children":["Try the same-course comparison",["$","$L5",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$6:props:children:2:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-right","aria-hidden":"true"}]]}]]}]]}]
20:["$","section","2-Unweighted does not mean every row has equal influence",{"className":"article-section","id":"section-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Unweighted does not mean every row has equal influence"}],[["$","p","A semester-long half-credit elective and a full-year one-credit course can have different weights in the GPA even when neither has a rigor bonus. Use credit-weighted quality points when the school assigns unequal credit.",{"children":"A semester-long half-credit elective and a full-year one-credit course can have different weights in the GPA even when neither has a rigor bonus. Use credit-weighted quality points when the school assigns unequal credit."}],["$","p","With one 0.5-credit A and one 1-credit B, quality points are 2 + 3 and credits are 1.5, producing 3.33 on the example base scale. Averaging the two letter-grade values equally would produce 3.50 and answer a different question.",{"children":"With one 0.5-credit A and one 1-credit B, quality points are 2 + 3 and credits are 1.5, producing 3.33 on the example base scale. Averaging the two letter-grade values equally would produce 3.50 and answer a different question."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined"]}]
21:["$","section","3-Why subtracting a fixed amount does not reliably convert GPA",{"className":"article-section","id":"section-4","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Why subtracting a fixed amount does not reliably convert GPA"}],[["$","p","A weighted total by itself does not identify the number of advanced courses, their credit weights or the bonus policy. Two transcripts can reach the same weighted average through different combinations. Subtracting 0.5 or 1.0 from the total assumes information you do not have.",{"children":"A weighted total by itself does not identify the number of advanced courses, their credit weights or the bonus policy. Two transcripts can reach the same weighted average through different combinations. Subtracting 0.5 or 1.0 from the total assumes information you do not have."}],["$","p","A useful conversion workflow starts with course-level grades and credits, removes only the bonuses actually applied, and recalculates the average. If that record is unavailable, describe the conversion as unknown rather than issuing a precise-looking answer.",{"children":"A useful conversion workflow starts with course-level grades and credits, removes only the bonuses actually applied, and recalculates the average. If that record is unavailable, describe the conversion as unknown rather than issuing a precise-looking answer."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined"]}]
22:["$","section","4-School reporting and college recalculation are separate",{"className":"article-section","id":"section-5","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"School reporting and college recalculation are separate"}],[["$","p","A college can use its own definition of the academic record. UC, for example, publishes an A–G course window, honors eligibility and caps for its requirement GPA. That is more specific than a generic high-school weighted average. Do not rename an ordinary 5-point estimate as a UC result.",{"children":"A college can use its own definition of the academic record. UC, for example, publishes an A–G course window, honors eligibility and caps for its requirement GPA. That is more specific than a generic high-school weighted average. Do not rename an ordinary 5-point estimate as a UC result."}],["$","p","For an application field, follow the current form instructions and the official transcript. Include the relevant scale/weighting information when requested. Do not substitute a more flattering self-calculated number for the record the form asks you to report.",{"children":"For an application field, follow the current form instructions and the official transcript. Include the relevant scale/weighting information when requested. Do not substitute a more flattering self-calculated number for the record the form asks you to report."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined"]}]
23:["$","section","5-Use GPA to plan a better question",{"className":"article-section","id":"section-6","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Use GPA to plan a better question"}],[["$","p","If your concern is a recent low grade, identify whether it is a school-GPA issue, a college eligibility condition or a repeat-course policy question. Each needs different evidence. A school counsellor can explain the local weighting rules, while the college controls its admissions method.",{"children":"If your concern is a recent low grade, identify whether it is a school-GPA issue, a college eligibility condition or a repeat-course policy question. Each needs different evidence. A school counsellor can explain the local weighting rules, while the college controls its admissions method."}],["$","p","When comparing future courses, consider prerequisites, interest, learning support and a manageable workload alongside the bonus. A larger numerical boost is not a guarantee that a more demanding schedule is the right choice.",{"children":"When comparing future courses, consider prerequisites, interest, learning support and a manageable workload alongside the bonus. A larger numerical boost is not a guarantee that a more demanding schedule is the right choice."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined",["$","$L27",null,{"id":"guides/weighted-vs-unweighted-gpa:section-6","title":"Check your progress","items":["Both GPAs use the same course set.","Credits and grade scale are understood.","School weighting policy is documented.","Application reporting instructions are followed.","A named college calculation is not confused with school GPA."]}],"$undefined",["$","nav",null,{"className":"answer-links","aria-label":"Next steps for Use GPA to plan a better question","children":[["$","$L4","/tools/weighted-gpa-calculator/",{"href":"/tools/weighted-gpa-calculator/","children":["Calculate weighted and unweighted GPA",["$","$L5",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$6:props:children:2:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-right","aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","$L4","/tools/gpa-calculator-with-retakes/",{"href":"/tools/gpa-calculator-with-retakes/","children":["Model course retakes",["$","$L5",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$6:props:children:2:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-right","aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","$L4","/guides/ap-vs-clep/",{"href":"/guides/ap-vs-clep/","children":["Understand AP versus CLEP",["$","$L5",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$6:props:children:2:props:children:1:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-right","aria-hidden":"true"}]]}]]}]]}]
